{
  "diagnosis": {
    "instruction": "Diagnose from trusted facts and attributable evidence. Distinguish tool-check facts from hypotheses. Choose exactly one currently executable component using observed history, expected effect and cost; do not preassign components by error class.",
    "rules": [
      {
        "id": "zero_delta_unconfirmed",
        "when": true,
        "instruction": "Zero observed delta does not establish mechanism success or exhaustion. Distinguish diagnostic training windows from the supplied paired internal-development evidence; inspect implementation, activation opportunities, target errors, costs and counter-evidence before deciding whether the same target remains justified. Do not infer an isolated causal effect."
      },
      {
        "id": "cross_task_contamination_priority",
        "when": true,
        "instruction": "Identifiers or notes from other task_ids are evidence to inspect artifact provenance and scoping. Treat unintended scope leakage as a hypothesis, not proof or an automatic HARNESS choice: reusable cross-task content can be legitimate. Compare actual H consumption, A content and available MODEL training signal before routing; in branch operations retain the accepted component."
      },
      {
        "id": "memory_completion_assertion_priority",
        "when": true,
        "instruction": "Finalization based on a summary completion assertion without confirming tool observations is evidence to investigate the memory/submission mechanism. Check full action-observation context and task conditions, compare feasible remedies and counter-evidence, and do not force HARNESS solely from this label. Branch operations only implement the frozen decision."
      },
      {
        "id": "tool_argument_misuse_priority",
        "when": true,
        "instruction": "Malformed entity lookup or collection replacement patterns are evidence to investigate tool-use behavior. Inspect schema, action-observation pairs, content availability, current execution mechanisms and eligible training signal. They do not by themselves establish component ownership or require a fixed priority. Respect the operation and accepted decision."
      }
    ]
  },
  "evidence": {
    "field_chars": 1200,
    "fields": [
      "task_id",
      "domain",
      "error_type",
      "terminal_reward",
      "verification",
      "model_ref"
    ],
    "filter": true,
    "fragments": {
      "after": 1,
      "anchor": {
        "any": [
          {
            "op": "contains",
            "path": "message.content",
            "value": "Error"
          },
          {
            "op": "contains",
            "path": "message.content",
            "value": "error"
          }
        ]
      },
      "before": 1,
      "fallback": "tail",
      "max_messages": 6,
      "message_chars": 2000,
      "retain_tools": []
    },
    "group_by": [
      "domain",
      "error_type"
    ],
    "max_chars": 32000,
    "max_items": 8,
    "score_rules": [],
    "sort": [
      {
        "direction": "desc",
        "field": "generation"
      }
    ]
  },
  "experience": {
    "applicability": [],
    "exclude_failed": true,
    "failure": [],
    "field_chars": 1600,
    "fields": [
      "generation",
      "chosen_action",
      "requested_change",
      "actual_change",
      "observed_performance_delta",
      "cost_before",
      "cost_after",
      "versions"
    ],
    "filter": true,
    "group_by": [
      "chosen_action"
    ],
    "max_chars": 24000,
    "max_items": 8,
    "require_applicable": false,
    "score_rules": [],
    "sort": [
      {
        "direction": "desc",
        "field": "generation"
      }
    ]
  },
  "schema_version": 1,
  "self_update": {
    "instruction": "### Role\nYou are the experimental RSI Meta-Agent. After a real Task intervention and its paired reevaluation, improve the procedure you use to improve Task agents. Your editable state is G: Workflow, Action, and Memory. Task state remains T=(M,H,A).\n\n### Objective and Acceptance Contract\nProduce one minimal, evidence-grounded, substantive G update, publishable with its principle memory K, and usable by the immediately following routing operation. This experiment requires an update at every Meta self-update boundary. Return status=\"UPDATED\". NO_CHANGE, an unchanged policy with a new label, and a memory-only addition without a changed operative G rule do not satisfy this contract.\n\nA required update is not a required performance gain. Preserve negative results, uncertainty, and counterexamples. Do not invent a lesson, causal claim, success, or version merely to pass validation. If the evidence supports only a local procedural weakness or an evidence gap, improve that specific inspection, comparison, decision, or recovery procedure and state the limited scope. If no lawful grounded candidate can be formed, report the exact impediment through the existing bounded repair/failure path; never submit fabricated success.\n\n### Inputs\nUse the frozen pre-intervention expectation, exact Task component decision and implemented diff, loading receipts, controller-bound paired outcome, current G/K, relevant historical experiences and counterexamples, capability catalog, and actual output schema. Full permitted paired traces are available at meta_input/internal_dev_comparison.json. Use exact citation IDs from principle_contract.evidence_ids; JSON field names such as trusted_facts.performance and checks.candidate_contract are not source IDs.\n\n### Procedure\n1. **Recover the prospective expectation.** Read the expectation recorded before the Task intervention. Do not change it. Historical absence remains not_recorded. Identify the chosen M/H/A component, intended mechanism, target errors, observable behavior, and cost prediction.\n2. **Assess the actual transition.** Verify matching tasks, resets, scoring, and budgets. Separately assess implementation, activation opportunities/events, and outcome. Report denominators and distinguish binary success, terminal reward, native partial metrics, and costs. Compare the same development tasks; different training windows provide context only. A missing activation event is uncertainty, not proof of absence.\n3. **Identify one improvement to the improvement procedure.** State the observed discrepancy or information gap, the specific limitation of current G, and a conditional procedural correction. Inspect the existing rule before claiming the correction is new. An existing general caution may need an executable check, a more precise trigger, an evidence requirement, or a changed inspection order; repeating its wording is insufficient. Do not infer universal component rankings or model exhaustion from one failed intervention.\n4. **Maintain principle memory.** Retrieve and compare active principles and counterexamples. Use at least one justified ADD, MERGE, REVISE, or RETIRE operation under the real schema. ADD begins tentative; one transition is not multiple independent examples. Retain failures and raw history. Retirement must remove the principle's active effect from G. This is Meta K, never Task A or SFT data.\n5. **Implement the smallest operative G change.** Change at least one legal instruction or executable configuration target in instructions.md, context.json, workflow.json, or evolution.json. Each change must bind an active principle ID to an exact target, a procedural purpose, and an observable next-call event; register the target in the principle's g_targets. Specify what the next routing must inspect, compare, require, or order differently. Preserve autonomous single-component M/H/A selection. Do not predetermine its answer.\n6. **Validate and hand off.** Check exact schema, evidence IDs, target legality, principle revisions, and consistency of G/K. Use existing bounded repair for concrete errors. The controller commits validated G/K atomically. The next routing, Task modification request, and self-update must load that committed identity. A proposed or committed file is not proof of subsequent use.\n\n### Boundaries\nDo not change the model, provider, Codex binary, deployment, interpreter, permissions, scoring, task splits, hidden answers, original receipts, or external budgets. Do not put probe solutions into Task assets or training. Do not edit the fixed self_update_protocol.md. Only declared G targets and principle operations are editable. No additional Task rollout or component search is authorized by this self-update operation. A change may be small but must change an applicable instruction, evidence requirement, decision rule, or executable workflow; formatting and timestamps are invalid substitutes.\n\n### Output\nReturn the exact FiveStageMetaUpdate business schema. harness is complete instructions.md text. bundle_files contains complete replacement text for changed legal G files, not principles.json or self_update_protocol.md. The materializer derives principles.json from five_stage.principle_operations. Set status=\"UPDATED\" and five_stage.no_change_reason=null. Include implementation, activation, outcome, principle_operations, harness_bindings, and subsequent_verification with correct expectation/outcome identities. Describe the conditional lesson and exact old/new procedure concisely in rationale and changed_rules.\n\nUse native Codex tools to write the complete request-bound candidate envelope, then compute its actual byte hash and return the designated small delivery receipt. Do not invent schema fields, tool results, hashes, or commit receipts.\n\n### Subsequent Use and Reporting\nThe next routing must consult the newly committed G/K, apply relevant principles, and list the actually used principle IDs. Record loading identity, the requested observable procedure, and the resulting component choice separately. Report four separate facts: principle memory changed; operative G changed; new G was used by the next real Meta request; Task performance changed. This operation can establish the first three only when their evidence exists; it cannot declare a performance improvement before reevaluation.\n",
    "rules": [
      {
        "id": "new_failure_class_priority",
        "when": true,
        "instruction": "New error classes deserve investigation, including whether the change was implemented, whether the opportunity occurred, and whether errors are infrastructural or task-level. Different training windows cannot establish newly caused errors. Do not force a component from an error label or assume a newly sampled error was absent from all earlier behavior."
      },
      {
        "id": "prompt_growth_cost_observation",
        "when": true,
        "instruction": "Include measured cost changes (model_calls, input_tokens, wall_time_seconds) in the intended-vs-observed comparison. Cost changes are observations, not validation: a prompt-rule addition that coincides with higher per-call input tokens and higher total cost, or a config change that coincides with lower total cost only through fewer model calls while per-call input tokens do not fall, is a cost observation to report in the next expected-cost estimate; do not assert causality from it."
      },
      {
        "id": "repeated_zero_delta_requires_new_evidence",
        "when": true,
        "instruction": "Non-positive outcomes require honest attribution and do not prove component exhaustion. Ground the mandatory G change in an observed procedural weakness or evidence gap; no fabricated gain or predetermined component."
      },
      {
        "id": "mechanism_removal_is_observation",
        "when": true,
        "instruction": "A removed mechanism or fewer model calls can establish implementation/cost observations when supported by actual events; they do not establish task success, causal attribution, or exhaustion of the targeted part. Check paired task outcomes and opportunities separately. Further work on that part requires evidence, not automatic exclusion or compulsory redirection."
      },
      {
        "id": "first_turn_activation_audit",
        "when": true,
        "instruction": "When the evaluated intervention changed the initial system prompt or other first-turn behavior, do not default activation to unknown if paired before/after traces exist. For each paired key, compare the first non-system action and its observation before vs after; mark per-pair activated/not_activated/no_opportunity; and separate implementation (loaded identity) from activation (observable behavior change) from outcome (success/native partial). Report model_calls, tool_call_denominator, and input/output token deltas per pair; a cost increase driven by more calls while per-call tokens stay flat is a cost observation for the next expected-cost estimate, not validation. Do not reuse the identical target without new activation evidence."
      }
    ]
  },
